/S /H2
/P 126 0 R
/T (The introduction of the BRSR framework by the Securities and Exchange Board of India marked a significant step towards standardising sustainability disclosures for listed companies. By aligning reporting requirements with the National Guidelines on Responsible Business Conduct, BRSR seeks to improve transparency, accountability, and comparability across environmental, social, and governance dimensions. Despite these advances, challenges remain in translating disclosed information into actionable insight. BRSR reports are typically extensive and descriptive, and stakeholders often lack structured frameworks to interpret performance across companies or over time. As a result, the analytical potential of BRSR data remains underutilised. This report addresses this gap by applying a comparative, indicator-based methodology to BRSR disclosures within the automobile manufacturing sector. By focusing on intensity-based metrics, peer benchmarking, and integrated ESG interpretation, the study aims to demonstrate how BRSR data can be used to assess performance outcomes rather than disclosure volume. The analysis is intended not as a ranking exercise, but as a diagnostic assessment that highlights relative strengths, structural gaps, and cross-dimensional trade-offs. In doing so, the report seeks to contribute to ongoing discussions on how sustainability reporting in India can evolve from a compliance-oriented exercise to a more effective tool for performance evaluation and strategic decision-making.)
/E (The introduction of the BRSR framework by the Securities and Exchange Board of India marked a significant step towards standardising sustainability disclosures for listed companies. By aligning reporting requirements with the National Guidelines on Responsible Business Conduct, BRSR seeks to improve transparency, accountability, and comparability across environmental, social, and governance dimensions. Despite these advances, challenges remain in translating disclosed information into actionable insight. BRSR reports are typically extensive and descriptive, and stakeholders often lack structured frameworks to interpret performance across companies or over time. As a result, the analytical potential of BRSR data remains underutilised. This report addresses this gap by applying a comparative, indicator-based methodology to BRSR disclosures within the automobile manufacturing sector. By focusing on intensity-based metrics, peer benchmarking, and integrated ESG interpretation, the study aims to demonstrate how BRSR data can be used to assess performance outcomes rather than disclosure volume. The analysis is intended not as a ranking exercise, but as a diagnostic assessment that highlights relative strengths, structural gaps, and cross-dimensional trade-offs. In doing so, the report seeks to contribute to ongoing discussions on how sustainability reporting in India can evolve from a compliance-oriented exercise to a more effective tool for performance evaluation and strategic decision-making.)
/Pg 12 0 R
/K [2 3 4 5]
>>
endobj
