/T (The analysis reveals that well-being expenditure remains modest across the peer group, with only limited variation between firms. Even among relatively stronger performers, expenditure levels suggest that workforce well-being is not yet a core strategic priority when viewed in relation to overall business scale. This raises questions about whether current well-being initiatives are primarily compliance-driven or represent sustained investment in human capital. The findings also highlight a potential disconnect between environmental ambition and social investment. Firms demonstrating strong environmental efficiency do not necessarily exhibit higher well-being expenditure, reinforcing the observation that sustainability efforts are often pursued in silos rather than through an integrated approach.)
