/T (The analysis shows that reductions in GHG emission intensity are not consistently driven by renewable energy adoption alone. Companies demonstrating lower emission intensity often benefit from process efficiency, operational optimisation, and scale effects. Industry-wide sustainability strategies should therefore place greater emphasis on energy productivity and emission efficiency metrics rather than relying predominantly on renewable energy share as a proxy for decarbonisation. Industry bodies and regulators could encourage more consistent reporting of intensity-based metrics and promote benchmarking of efficiency improvements across firms. This would enable stakeholders to distinguish between structural efficiency gains and symbolic sustainability actions.)
/E (The analysis shows that reductions in GHG emission intensity are not consistently driven by renewable energy adoption alone. Companies demonstrating lower emission intensity often benefit from process efficiency, operational optimisation, and scale effects. Industry-wide sustainability strategies should therefore place greater emphasis on energy productivity and emission efficiency metrics rather than relying predominantly on renewable energy share as a proxy for decarbonisation. Industry bodies and regulators could encourage more consistent reporting of intensity-based metrics and promote benchmarking of efficiency improvements across firms. This would enable stakeholders to distinguish between structural efficiency gains and symbolic sustainability actions.)
/Pg 27 0 R
/K [3 4]
>>
endobj
