<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 146 0 R
/E (While this study provides a structured and comparative assessment of sustainability performance using BRSR disclosures, certain limitations should be acknowledged.)
/Pg 32 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
356 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 146 0 R
/E (First, the analysis relies entirely on self-reported data disclosed under the BRSR framework. Although BRSR has improved standardisation and transparency, variations in reporting practices, boundary definitions, and data assurance levels may affect comparability. The study does not independently verify disclosed data and assumes consistency in reporting quality across firms.)
/Pg 32 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
357 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 146 0 R
/E (Second, the scope of analysis is limited to four automobile manufacturers, selected based on data availability and sectoral relevance. While this allows for focused peer benchmarking, the findings may not be fully representative of the broader automobile value chain, including component manufacturers, suppliers, and downstream actors.)
/Pg 32 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
358 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 146 0 R
/E <>
/Pg 32 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
359 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 146 0 R
/E (Finally, the normalisation and scoring approach adopted in this study reflects relative performance within the selected peer group, not absolute sustainability adequacy. A higher score indicates stronger performance compared to peers, but does not imply alignment with science-based targets or best-in-class global benchmarks.)
/Pg 32 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
360 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 146 0 R
/E (Despite these limitations, the methodology provides a transparent and replicable framework for extracting analytical value from BRSR disclosures and can be extended to larger samples, additional sectors, or more granular indicators as data quality improves.)
/Pg 32 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
