/T (CONCLUSION)
/E (CONCLUSION)
/Pg 33 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
363 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 147 0 R
/E (This report set out to examine whether BRSR disclosures can be used not only as compliance documents but also as tools for comparative performance assessment and strategic insight. By applying an indicator-based, peer-benchmarked approach to environmental and social data disclosed by leading automobile manufacturers, the study demonstrates that meaningful insights can be derived from BRSR when data are analysed systematically rather than read descriptively.)
/Pg 33 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
364 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 147 0 R
/E <>
/Pg 33 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
365 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 147 0 R
/E (The integrated ESG analysis further highlights that sustainability leadership is less about excelling in isolated indicators and more about achieving balanced and consistent outcomes across dimensions. Firms that prioritise environmental efficiency without parallel investment in social performance may face hidden operational and reputational risks, while firms with stronger social outcomes but weaker environmental efficiency may encounter transition-related challenges.)
/Pg 33 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
366 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 147 0 R
/E (At a broader level, the study suggests that the next phase of BRSR evolution should focus on interpretability and outcome quality, rather than on expanding disclosure volume alone. For companies, this implies embedding sustainability metrics into core operational and strategic decision-making. For regulators and stakeholders, it highlights the value of benchmarking, intensity-based indicators, and integrated analysis in improving the usefulness of sustainability reporting.)
/Pg 33 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
